Q: How to check if a function is a constructor A: <p>In the old days you just had to test for functions with a prototype property:</p>
<pre><code>typeof value=='function' &amp;&amp; 'prototype' in value// function with prototype property
</code></pre>
<p>But those were the days before bound and generator functions. For example the above will return <code>false</code> for constructors created with <code>Function.prototype.bind()</code> and <code>true</code> for generator functions (which can't construct per the ES7 spec).</p>
<p>So these days there are better ways, like this cross-browser solution that also works with bound and generator functions. Acknowledgement to Ryan Hanekamp for his answer which I incorporated in my answer below. This works in both modern and old ES3+ browsers including Node.js and IE:</p>
<pre><code>var isConstructor = typeof Proxy=='function'?
function(value) {// Works in modern environments that support Proxy (ES6+)
    if (typeof value=='function') {// Test for [[Construct]] internal method
        try {// Throws if `value` is not a constructor function
            var success = new new Proxy(value, {construct:Object});
        }
        catch(e) {
        }
    }
    return !!success
}:
function(value) {// Tested in pre-ES6 JS including Internet Explorer 6 and up
    // Since generators construct in pre-ES7 JS, include them here.
    var is_function = typeof value=='function';

    // In IE, host objects with a prototype property can construct too.
    var has_proto_prop = value===Object(value) &amp;&amp; 'prototype' in value;

    // If function has a prototype property and is not a built-in
    // function starting with lowercase, it is probably a constructor
    var success = is_function &amp;&amp; has_proto_prop &amp;&amp; !/^\s*\w+ [a-z_]\w*\(\) {\s*\[native c/.test(value);

    // In IE, value could be a constructor where typeof value!='function'
    // Or no prototype property means value might be a bound constructor
    if (has_proto_prop? !is_function: is_function) {
        try {
            // instanceof can be used to test for constructors because it
            // throws when used on functions with no prototype property.
            // With bound functions it uses prototype property of target.
            success = {} instanceof value || true
        }
        catch(e) {
        }
    }
    return success
}
isConstructor(window)//false
isConstructor(alert)//false
isConstructor(XMLHttpRequest)//true
isConstructor(Array.bind())//true
isConstructor(function myFunc(){})//true
isConstructor(function* myGenerator(){})//false
// Caveat: IE returns false-positives on bound host objects
isConstructor(isNaN.bind())//false (works in IE since isNaN isn't a host object)
isConstructor(alert.bind())//false (however in IE this returns true (false-positive))
</code></pre>

Q: Vue doesn&#39;t load styles in root A: <p>Vue is not blocking the request. The issue is that the font is never actually used, so the browser doesn’t request it.</p>
<p>Browsers only download a font declared with @font-face when some element uses that font-family. If nothing references &quot;FontAwesome&quot;, Firefox (and most browsers) will not make a network request for the font files.</p>
<p>In your example you only declare the font:</p>
<pre><code>
@font-face {
  font-family: &quot;FontAwesome&quot;;
  src: url(&quot;/fonts/fa-regular-400.woff2&quot;) format(&quot;woff2&quot;),
       url(&quot;/fonts/fa-solid-900.woof2&quot;) format(&quot;woff2&quot;),
       url(&quot;/fonts/fa-brands-400.woff2&quot;) format(&quot;woff2&quot;);
}
</code></pre>
<p>but you never apply it anywhere, so the browser has no reason to load it.</p>
<p>Add a rule that actually uses the font, for example:</p>
<pre><code>body {
  font-family: &quot;FontAwesome&quot;;
}
</code></pre>
<p>or with an icon element:</p>
<pre><code>
.fa {
  font-family: &quot;FontAwesome&quot;;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Once the font is referenced by a rendered element, the browser will request the .woff2 files.</p>
<p>Another issue in your snippet</p>
<p>You also have a typo: <strong>fa-solid-900.woof2</strong><br />
should be: <strong>fa-solid-900.woff2</strong></p>
<p>If that path were used it would fail to load.</p>
<p>About /public in Vue</p>
<p>If you are using Vite or Vue CLI, files inside /public are served from the root path. So:</p>
<p><strong>public/fonts/fa-regular-400.woff2</strong></p>
<p>is correctly referenced as:</p>
<p><strong>/fonts/fa-regular-400.woff2</strong></p>
<p>No additional configuration is required.</p>

Q: React Leaflet(js) Position Issue with ImageOverlay A: <p>I can see the problem clearly in the screenshot — markers are in the upper half, the map image is pushed to the lower half, and the image renders on top of the markers. There are several interacting issues here:</p>
<p>1. Pixel origin desync (the stacking/offset problem)</p>
<p>This is the main issue. You have multiple competing effects all trying to control the map's viewport, plus both ref={mapRef} and whenCreated setting the map reference. Leaflet's internal pixel origin gets out of sync when  invalidateSize + fitBounds are called from different effects at different times, especially combined with zoomAnimation: false and an initial zoom={-3}.</p>
<p>Fix: Remove whenCreated, remove the separate bounds effect, and consolidate into a single ResizeObserver effect:</p>
<p>&lt;MapContainer</p>
<pre><code>ref={mapRef}

crs={L.CRS.Simple}

center={center}

zoom={-2}

minZoom={minZoom ?? undefined}

maxZoom={3}

zoomSnap={0}

zoomDelta={0.25}

zoomControl={false}

attributionControl={false}

bounceAtZoomLimits={false}

// keep zoomAnimation and fadeAnimation default (true)

inertia={false}

maxBoundsViscosity={1.0}

style={{ position: &quot;absolute&quot;, inset: 0, height: &quot;100%&quot;, width: &quot;100%&quot; }}
</code></pre>
<p>&gt;</p>
<p>Remove the whenCreated prop entirely — ref already gives you the map instance. Then merge your two useEffects (the one watching bounds and the ResizeObserver one) into one:</p>
<p>useEffect(() =&gt; {</p>
<pre><code>if (!wrapRef.current || !mapRef.current) return;

const map = mapRef.current;



let raf = 0;

const reframe = () =\&gt; {

  cancelAnimationFrame(raf);

  raf = requestAnimationFrame(() =\&gt; {

    map.invalidateSize({ animate: false });

    map.fitBounds(bounds, { animate: false, padding: \[0, 0\] });

    map.setMaxBounds(bounds);

    const fitZoom = map.getBoundsZoom(bounds, false);

    map.setMinZoom(fitZoom);

  });

};



// Initial frame + resize tracking

reframe();

const ro = new ResizeObserver(reframe);

ro.observe(wrapRef.current);



return () =\&gt; {

  cancelAnimationFrame(raf);

  ro.disconnect();

};
</code></pre>
<p>}, [bounds]);</p>
<p>And remove the separate useEffect that watches [bounds] and calls fitBounds/setMaxBounds.</p>
<p>2. Image renders on top of markers (z-ordering)</p>
<p>Your imagePane has zIndex: 200, which is below the default markerPane (600) — so markers should be on top. However, there's a competing problem: the shadow overlay div:</p>
<p>&lt;div className=&quot;absolute inset-0 shadow-[inset_0_0_120px_rgba(0,0,0,0.85)]&quot; /&gt;</p>
<p>This is a sibling of MapContainer positioned with absolute inset-0, so it sits on top of the entire Leaflet map in the DOM stacking order, visually covering markers. Move this inside the map as a Leaflet pane, or apply the shadow to the wrapper instead:</p>
<p>&lt;div ref={wrapRef} className={containerClass} style={{</p>
<pre><code>...containerStyle,

boxShadow: &quot;inset 0 0 120px rgba(0,0,0,0.85)&quot;,
</code></pre>
<p>}}&gt;</p>
<pre><code>{/\* remove the separate shadow div \*/}
</code></pre>
<p>Additionally, make sure you're not putting markers inside imagePane. Your current code places markers outside any  &lt;Pane&gt;, so they default to the markerPane (z-index 600). That's correct — just verify no CSS is overriding this.</p>
<p>3. Syntax error</p>
<p>&lt;div className=&quot;pt-2 flex gap-2&quot;</p>
<p>This is missing the closing &gt;. Should be:</p>
<p>&lt;div className=&quot;pt-2 flex gap-2&quot;&gt;</p>
<p>This would cause a build error or broken rendering of the marker editor modal.</p>
<p>4. Debug dot BR is off by 80px</p>
<p>{ id: &quot;dbg-br&quot;, px: W, py: H - 80, ... }</p>
<p>The fact that you needed H - 80 to make &quot;bottom-right&quot; appear correct is a symptom of the pixel origin desync in issue</p>
<p>#1. Once you fix the viewport initialization, this should just be py: H.</p>
<hr />
<p>In summary: The root cause is Leaflet's pixel origin getting confused by multiple racing effects that all call invalidateSize/fitBounds at different times during mount. Consolidate them into one, drop whenCreated, and remove the  absolute-positioned shadow div that covers the map. The coordinate math and calibration logic itself looks correct.</p>
